{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Patch set 1 → 2: Verified -1 triaged and fixed\n\n**Patch set 1 votes:** LLM-Review **+1** (PASS, 4 advisory non-blocking findings),\nVerified **-1**.\n\n**Triage.** Not a flake: all four pytest jobs (ubuntu py3.11/3.12/3.13, macos py3.13) failed\nin 26-47s — too fast to be the suite, and identical across platforms, so deterministic. The\nlog showed a generated-doc gate, not a test:\n\n    docs/env-vars.md is stale — regenerate it with\n      python scripts/gen_env_registry.py\n\n**Cause.** `bridge_repair._actor()` falls back to `os.environ.get(\"USER\")` (mirroring\n`tracker_maintenance._actor`), and `docs/env-vars.md` is a generated registry of every\nenv-var read under `src/rebar`. Adding a second reader of `USER` made the checked-in table\nstale. The regenerated diff is exactly the expected one line:\n\n    -| `USER` | `src/rebar/_commands/tracker_maintenance.py` |  |\n    +| `USER` | `src/rebar/_commands/bridge_repair.py`, `src/rebar/_commands/tracker_maintenance.py` |  |\n\n**My miss.** I checked `gen_cli_reference.py --check` before pushing but not the other three\ngenerators. Now verified all four are clean — `gen_adr_index`, `gen_cli_reference`,\n`gen_env_registry`, `gen_mcp_reference` — and that is the check to run for any change adding\nan env-var read, a CLI flag, an MCP tool, or an ADR.\n\nPatch set 2 pushed (b430b22bc9, Change-Id preserved). No behavioural change from patch set 1 —\none regenerated doc line. Awaiting fresh votes."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786561926447645001,"uuid":"d2e38ddc-d6d7-4463-860f-b42cc1636c3e"}